00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:54,040
and they ground it in a flower.

253
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:59,160
In the colonies, and for centuries that followed,

254
00:16:59,260 --> 00:17:01,060
corn was harvested by hand.

255
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:06,160
The corn knife with an 18-inch blade

256
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:08,660
was one of the original methods of cutting the stalks.

257
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:14,340
When farmers were picking only the ears,

258
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:17,800
handheld hooks, or pegs made of iron and bone,

259
00:17:17,900 --> 00:17:21,440
were the standard tools.

260
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:25,340
This is a bone peg that was probably used in 1890.

261
00:17:25,440 --> 00:17:28,860
They used anything that was sharp to open up the shuck

262
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:31,760
at that time.

263
00:17:31,860 --> 00:17:34,860
Gloves with embedded hooks later replaced the peg.

264
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:37,200
People in rural communities are very innovative,

265
00:17:37,300 --> 00:17:40,800
and so they were always looking for some way to do it better,

266
00:17:40,801 --> 00:17:42,460
better, to do it faster.

267
00:17:42,461 --> 00:17:46,200
The hooks and pegs allowed for faster harvesting because they

268
00:17:46,201 --> 00:17:50,840
could rip the shuck away from the ear, snap it, and harvest

269
00:17:50,841 --> 00:17:55,540
it much quicker than just by hand.

270
00:17:55,541 --> 00:17:58,760
Even today, many rural communities still celebrate

271
00:17:58,761 --> 00:18:02,160
the days of hand harvesting with husking bee competitions.

272
00:18:05,160 --> 00:18:08,760
Ted Martin is a former Nebraska state champion.

273
00:18:08,761 --> 00:18:12,760
Well, you grab the ear towards the end like this

274
00:18:12,761 --> 00:18:16,760
and come in with the hook, crossways, open up the shucks,

275
00:18:16,761 --> 00:18:21,760
then you peel them back and you snap the ear out and throw it.

276
00:18:21,761 --> 00:18:25,760
And it's all one motion, really.

277
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:32,760
A fast husker could generally harvest about 100 bushels

278
00:18:32,761 --> 00:18:34,760
or one acre of corn per day.

279
00:18:38,760 --> 00:18:40,760
Once the whole corn ears were dry,

280
00:18:40,761 --> 00:18:43,760
the kernels had to be removed.

281
00:18:43,761 --> 00:18:45,760
For decades, nearly every farm was equipped

282
00:18:45,761 --> 00:18:49,760
with a hand-operated corn sheller.

283
00:18:49,761 --> 00:18:51,760
Okay, we've got a couple cobs of corn,

284
00:18:51,761 --> 00:18:55,760
and we're looking at a hand-powered corn sheller

285
00:18:55,761 --> 00:18:57,760
that's about 100 years old,

286
00:18:57,761 --> 00:18:59,760
and we're going to run a couple cobs through it.

287
00:18:59,761 --> 00:19:01,760
First, I'm going to crank it up.

288
00:19:01,761 --> 00:19:03,760
A couple plates inside the machine going

289
00:19:03,761 --> 00:19:07,760
in opposite directions are going to shear the kernels off.

290
00:19:07,761 --> 00:19:13,760
The corn kernels go down the bottom.

291
00:19:13,860 --> 00:19:16,800
The corn cobs come out the other end.

292
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:19,600
And there's a fan at the bottom that blows some of the chaff

293
00:19:19,700 --> 00:19:21,000
away as well.

294
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:27,720
Many farmers harvested corn for silage, or animal feed.

295
00:19:27,820 --> 00:19:30,120
This included the stalk as well as the ear.

296
00:19:33,020 --> 00:19:36,060
Silage was the first corn harvest to see mechanization

297
00:19:36,061 --> 00:19:39,120
to see mechanization in the form of the corn binder

298
00:19:39,121 --> 00:19:40,560
in the late 1800s.

299
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:47,000
On one Nebraska farm, Ron Lang operates an antique corn binder

300
00:19:47,001 --> 00:19:48,880
to harvest feed for his horses.

301
00:19:51,180 --> 00:19:53,240
It's an international corn binder.

302
00:19:54,240 --> 00:19:56,120
It cuts it and ties it.

303
00:19:57,940 --> 00:20:02,560
This mainly used to be used more for green corn

304
00:20:02,561 --> 00:20:04,720
than fill silos with it.

305
00:20:04,721 --> 00:20:07,700
They would cut the corn green and haul it in and fill your fiber.

306
00:20:10,400 --> 00:20:15,580
The binder both cuts and then automatically ties the stalks into bundles with twine.

307
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:21,440
The operator uses a foot clutch to engage a conveyor that drops the bundles in the field.

308
00:20:25,180 --> 00:20:32,900
Corn binders were usually followed by a crew of laborers who stood the bundles into larger upright stacks, known as shocks.

309
00:20:32,901 --> 00:20:35,200
See, the shotgun is for your winter feed,

310
00:20:35,300 --> 00:20:38,740
and it would mold and stuff on the ground.

311
00:20:38,840 --> 00:20:40,140
And the shotgun is for curing.

312
00:20:40,240 --> 00:20:44,040
You've got to shock it so the wind goes through it.

313
00:20:44,140 --> 00:20:45,940
Up through the Great Depression,

314
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:48,240
the meticulous hand harvest was still the only way

315
00:20:48,340 --> 00:20:49,560
to pick the ears directly.

316
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:56,560
The first mechanized corn pickers powered by internal combustion

317
00:20:56,660 --> 00:21:00,560
engines debuted in the late 1930s and would soon

318
00:21:00,561 --> 00:21:03,900
and would soon double productivity to 15 acres in one day.

319
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:14,680
The pickers selectively harvest only the corn ears,

320
00:21:14,681 --> 00:21:18,520
depositing the stalks back in the field.

321
00:21:18,521 --> 00:21:23,660
Farmer Randy Jensen uses a two-row 1960s model 237 John

322
00:21:23,661 --> 00:21:27,160
Deere picker to harvest 600 acres of corn each year.

323
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:35,560
The picker uses rollers to snap the ears off the stalks.

324
00:21:35,660 --> 00:21:38,660
The ears travel to a husking bed, where rubber rollers

325
00:21:38,760 --> 00:21:41,000
remove the husks.

326
00:21:41,100 --> 00:21:43,200
The clean ears then drop in a wagon.

327
00:21:43,300 --> 00:21:44,500
When you come to the end, you'll

328
00:21:44,600 --> 00:21:46,200
have a full wagon most of the time.

329
00:21:46,300 --> 00:21:48,020
And you'll have a guy at the other end,

330
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:51,520
and he'll switch wagons with you.

331
00:21:51,620 --> 00:21:54,160
And he'll take it home and run it to an elevator, which

332
00:21:54,260 --> 00:21:56,920
elevates it up into an ear corn crib.

333
00:21:56,921 --> 00:21:59,320
And basically, that's all you do.

334
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:06,560
While the mechanical pickers endured for decades,

335
00:22:06,660 --> 00:22:09,460
they couldn't shell, clean, and dry corn on the fly.

336
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:16,980
By the mid-1950s, there was an answer.

337
00:22:17,080 --> 00:22:20,240
The combine, which had long before mechanized the harvesting

338
00:22:20,340 --> 00:22:22,880
of other grain crops, was now advanced enough

339
00:22:22,980 --> 00:22:25,980
to produce clean corn kernels at a staggering pace.

340
00:22:26,920 --> 00:22:31,920
What began with two row designs grew to the 12 and even 16 row

341
00:22:32,020 --> 00:22:34,420
corn head combines of the 21st century.

342
00:22:34,520 --> 00:22:37,420
You're talking about the ability to harvest 4,000 or so

343
00:22:37,520 --> 00:22:41,360
bushels an hour, which translates to maybe 60 to 70

344
00:22:41,460 --> 00:22:46,380
pounds of grain, clean grain, every second that this machine

345
00:22:46,480 --> 00:22:47,780
can harvest.

346
00:22:47,880 --> 00:22:51,320
If we go back to working with a corn binder,

347
00:22:51,420 --> 00:22:54,220
we're talking about working maybe a little bit

348
00:22:54,221 --> 00:22:58,020
We're talking about working maybe eight acres a day,

349
00:22:58,120 --> 00:23:00,520
and now we're talking about hand picking.

350
00:23:00,620 --> 00:23:03,860
We're talking an acre or so a day, 100 bushels.

351
00:23:03,960 --> 00:23:06,760
Today, big farmers with large combines

352
00:23:06,860 --> 00:23:12,380
can do 40,000 bushels in a day with one combine.

353
00:23:12,480 --> 00:23:15,680
The American corn harvest relies on the size, speed,

354
00:23:15,780 --> 00:23:17,780
and sophistication of one machine.

355
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:23,420
But there is another uniquely American harvest

356
00:23:23,421 --> 00:23:26,660
that depends on a roll call of amphibious machines

357
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:31,060
to beat, comb, and rake a colorful fruit into submission.

358
00:23:36,760 --> 00:23:40,180
Every year in October, there is a fruit that must first go

359
00:23:40,280 --> 00:23:42,180
for a swim before it can be picked.

360
00:23:46,180 --> 00:23:49,580
Just days before harvesting, farmers in central Wisconsin

361
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:52,880
divert water from nearby reservoirs in order to flood

362
00:23:52,881 --> 00:23:58,580
thousands of acres of cranberries in up to two feet of water.

363
00:23:58,680 --> 00:24:00,380
This is called a wet harvest.

364
00:24:03,320 --> 00:24:06,420
The berries grow on short vines.

365
00:24:06,520 --> 00:24:08,520
When they mature, the fields are flooded

366
00:24:08,620 --> 00:24:09,740
to make harvesting easier.

367
00:24:14,540 --> 00:24:18,180
These water reel harvesters, also called beaters,

368
00:24:18,280 --> 00:24:21,180
will transform the marsh into a sea of red.

369
00:24:21,181 --> 00:24:24,580
The first guy that goes in the water is opening the bed.

370
00:24:24,680 --> 00:24:26,220
And then the rest of the guys behind him

371
00:24:26,320 --> 00:24:28,480
just keep following along behind him.

372
00:24:28,580 --> 00:24:30,260
What they're looking for in the water

373
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:32,720
is what we call a berry line, where

374
00:24:32,820 --> 00:24:35,300
you can see where the fruit's been picked clean

375
00:24:35,400 --> 00:24:36,860
and where the remaining fruit is.

376
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:38,260
So you try to line up on that.

377
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:41,740
And the idea is to not miss any fruit and beat it clean

378
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:44,000
or pick it clean.

379
00:24:44,100 --> 00:24:46,200
As the beaters move through the vine,

380
00:24:46,300 --> 00:24:49,100
they strip off the berries.

381
00:24:49,101 --> 00:24:52,000
Cranberries contain four air chambers.

382
00:24:52,100 --> 00:24:54,640
When freed from the vines, their natural buoyancy

383
00:24:54,740 --> 00:24:56,640
causes them to float.

384
00:24:56,740 --> 00:24:59,840
Cranberries are trained, meaning the vines lay

385
00:24:59,940 --> 00:25:01,340
a certain direction.

386
00:25:01,440 --> 00:25:03,440
A good analogy would be like petting a cat the right way

387
00:25:03,540 --> 00:25:04,640
and the wrong way.

388
00:25:04,740 --> 00:25:06,160
You pet it the right way, everything's nice.

389
00:25:06,260 --> 00:25:08,060
You go against the vines or against that cat,

390
00:25:08,160 --> 00:25:09,460
and every fur gets all ruffled up,

391
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:10,960
and it's not a very nice thing.

392
00:25:11,060 --> 00:25:12,960
Same thing can happen with cranberries.

393
00:25:13,060 --> 00:25:15,600
If we go against the vines, we'd rip them out of the ground,

394
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:18,500
and it would be a mess.

395
00:25:18,501 --> 00:25:23,740
Once the three-acre bed is beaten, the workers deploy a

396
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:27,540
cran boom to slowly coax the flotilla to one end of the marsh.

397
00:25:31,540 --> 00:25:35,720
Here, more than 80,000 pounds of berries are loaded by elevator

398
00:25:35,820 --> 00:25:36,820
and trucked out.

399
00:25:41,780 --> 00:25:44,200
At the Ocean Spray Receiving Station,

400
00:25:44,300 --> 00:25:48,500
the berries are offloaded into holding tanks.

401
00:25:48,501 --> 00:25:56,500
and then conveyed into the packing house, where they're

402
00:25:56,600 --> 00:26:00,040
cleaned and then sized.

403
00:26:00,140 --> 00:26:02,620
These cranberries are destined for processing,

404
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:06,480
meaning they will end up as juice, sauce, or even craisins,

405
00:26:06,580 --> 00:26:08,460
the cranberry equivalent of raisins.

406
00:26:10,680 --> 00:26:13,800
But long before cranberry juice and craisins dominated the

407
00:26:13,900 --> 00:26:17,460
market, Native Americans understood the power of the

408
00:26:17,461 --> 00:26:21,640
the power of the fresh berries, using them in medicines,

409
00:26:21,740 --> 00:26:25,140
poultices, and dyes.

410
00:26:25,240 --> 00:26:28,740
Dried berries were even pressed together with venison and fat

411
00:26:28,840 --> 00:26:31,540
to produce pemmican, a survival cake that

412
00:26:31,640 --> 00:26:33,080
kept for long periods of time.

413
00:26:36,640 --> 00:26:39,580
The first commercial growers in the 19th century

414
00:26:39,680 --> 00:26:42,980
recruited cheap field laborers, often women and children,

415
00:26:43,080 --> 00:26:47,460
to meticulously hand harvest the wild growing vines.

416
00:26:47,461 --> 00:26:53,720
When the men, women, and children would show up on the Cranberry Marsh, they were given a measure.

417
00:26:54,280 --> 00:27:01,220
Once they had a full measure, they would go over to the edge of the bed, dump the measure into the receptacle,

418
00:27:01,360 --> 00:27:03,500
and that picker was given a picking chip.

419
00:27:03,501 --> 00:27:10,280
That picking chip would be used for either cash or merchandise at the company store.

420
00:27:11,660 --> 00:27:17,020
In the 1890s, the first Cranberry Handrake replaced the picker's calloused fingers.

421
00:27:17,460 --> 00:27:19,460
quickly became the harvesting method of choice.

422
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:25,700
Growers eventually discovered that flooding the cranberry

423
00:27:25,800 --> 00:27:29,500
beds made hand raking much easier.

424
00:27:29,600 --> 00:27:30,600
This is the hand rake.

425
00:27:30,700 --> 00:27:33,800
It's got metal teeth with slots in it.

426
00:27:33,900 --> 00:27:38,020
As you rake, you go through the berries, through the vines

427
00:27:38,120 --> 00:27:39,220
like that.

428
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:42,260
The berries are floating on top of the water.

429
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:44,060
They're attached to the vines, but you still

430
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:45,460
got to take them off.

431
00:27:45,461 --> 00:27:49,660
And it's a back-breaking job if you have to do it all day long,

432
00:27:49,760 --> 00:27:50,860
especially if the vines aren't trained.

433
00:27:54,700 --> 00:27:58,200
In the 1950s, Wisconsinite Leonard Getzinger

434
00:27:58,300 --> 00:28:00,600
designed one of the first successful mechanical

435
00:28:00,700 --> 00:28:01,400
harvesters.

436
00:28:03,720 --> 00:28:07,520
The hand-pushed Getzinger picker uses retractable metal teeth

437
00:28:07,620 --> 00:28:09,720
to comb through the vines and pick berries.

438
00:28:09,721 --> 00:28:14,720
up until the 1970s, the majority of all cranberries

439
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:19,720
were sold as fresh fruit for traditional cooking.

440
00:28:19,820 --> 00:28:25,720
During the 1970s and 1980s, the juice market exploded,

441
00:28:25,820 --> 00:28:27,720
and the demand for cranberry juice

442
00:28:27,820 --> 00:28:31,720
far exceeded the demand for fresh cranberries.

443
00:28:31,820 --> 00:28:35,720
The whole industry shifted from fresh fruit

444
00:28:35,721 --> 00:28:42,320
the whole industry shifted from fresh fruit to processed fruit.

445
00:28:42,420 --> 00:28:45,060
Most farmers began wet harvesting with beaters

446
00:28:45,160 --> 00:28:48,160
to bring in a larger, yet less carefully handled crop.

447
00:28:51,060 --> 00:28:53,580
But one man feels there is still a better mousetrap

448
00:28:53,680 --> 00:28:57,380
for farmers who raise cranberries for processing.

449
00:28:57,480 --> 00:29:00,780
It's called the Ruby Slipper.

450
00:29:00,880 --> 00:29:02,680
That's a machine I invented about,

451
00:29:02,780 --> 00:29:05,720
I started working on it about seven years ago.

452
00:29:05,721 --> 00:29:10,020
trying to find a simpler, faster method

453
00:29:10,120 --> 00:29:12,520
to harvest cranberries that was easier on the fruit,

454
00:29:12,620 --> 00:29:15,460
easier on the vines.

455
00:29:15,560 --> 00:29:16,660
This is a lot gentler.

456
00:29:16,760 --> 00:29:18,960
The bar itself is traveling at a slower speed

457
00:29:19,060 --> 00:29:22,180
when it contacts the vine, but the ground speed of the machine

458
00:29:22,280 --> 00:29:23,280
is a lot faster.

459
00:29:26,480 --> 00:29:28,680
The ruby slipper is pulled through the marsh

460
00:29:28,780 --> 00:29:31,380
and requires no moving parts to remove the cranberries.

461
00:29:31,381 --> 00:29:37,380
of the machine, harvesting cranberries for juice or craisins

462
00:29:37,480 --> 00:29:38,780
is not a gentle affair.

463
00:29:44,760 --> 00:29:47,660
But there's another harvest more steeped in tradition

464
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:50,900
that demands a more delicate touch.

465
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:53,360
Abelman Brothers in Tomah, Wisconsin,

466
00:29:53,460 --> 00:29:56,400
is the world's largest producer of fresh cranberries.

467
00:29:56,401 --> 00:30:01,400
of fresh cranberries. This harvest is all about

468
00:30:01,500 --> 00:30:03,000
delivering the perfect berry.

469
00:30:05,700 --> 00:30:09,420
Habelman's harvesters utilize stainless steel teeth to comb

470
00:30:09,520 --> 00:30:14,220
the vines, much like the original Getzinger pickers.

471
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:17,380
But these more gentle modern harvesters use a series of

472
00:30:17,480 --> 00:30:20,920
paddles to free the berries from the vines.

473
00:30:21,020 --> 00:30:23,800
The berries never end up back in the water.

474
00:30:23,801 --> 00:30:26,700
They go right into the boats and then into the trucks.

475
00:30:26,701 --> 00:30:30,000
We can do about 10 acres a day with the two machines that we're

476
00:30:30,001 --> 00:30:30,840
using right now.

477
00:30:33,800 --> 00:30:36,460
When it's time for packing, the berries go through a separator

478
00:30:36,461 --> 00:30:40,760
line to remove discolored and poor quality berries.

479
00:30:40,761 --> 00:30:43,740
At the front end is an antique device that has never been

480
00:30:43,741 --> 00:30:48,440
upgraded or redesigned, the Bailey separator.

481
00:30:48,441 --> 00:30:52,500
Invented by the H.R. Bailey company in 1917,

482
00:30:52,501 --> 00:30:56,740
with the idea that only fresh cranberries will bounce.

483
00:30:56,840 --> 00:30:58,900
So they pass through a series of boards.

484
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:01,540
Good ones jump and land on a finish belt.

485
00:31:01,640 --> 00:31:03,940
The bad ones just kind of filter down the machine.

486
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:06,540
And a lot of companies have tried to come up with a better

487
00:31:06,640 --> 00:31:10,460
design, but there isn't any out there yet.

488
00:31:10,560 --> 00:31:12,460
The cranberries that pass Bailey's tests

489
00:31:12,560 --> 00:31:15,960
are then double-checked by more modern equipment.

490
00:31:16,060 --> 00:31:17,900
Two different electronic separators

491
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,400
use ultraviolet and fluorescent light

492
00:31:20,401 --> 00:31:23,400
light to detect inferior color and rot.

493
00:31:27,140 --> 00:31:30,500
After the antique and high-tech separators have their go,

494
00:31:30,501 --> 00:31:33,440
the final quality check is human.

495
00:31:33,441 --> 00:31:36,240
Any bad berries that have survived the gauntlet are weeded

496
00:31:36,241 --> 00:31:37,360
out before packaging.

497
00:31:42,760 --> 00:31:46,700
As the demand for healthier food and drink continues to spiral,

498
00:31:46,701 --> 00:31:50,360
a wild assortment of cranberry harvesting machines will continue

499
00:31:50,361 --> 00:31:52,160
the marshes of central Wisconsin.

500
00:31:55,460 --> 00:31:58,960
But picking berries in the Midwest autumn at low altitude

501
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:02,240
is a picnic compared to a hot, dangerous head in the clouds

502
00:32:02,340 --> 00:32:05,840
harvest, where one mistake could be fatal.

503
00:32:13,280 --> 00:32:17,380
In the low desert of Southern California's Coachella Valley,

504
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:20,360
there's an annual ritual that is perhaps the most difficult

505
00:32:20,361 --> 00:32:30,360
in America. This man is a palmero.

506
00:32:30,361 --> 00:32:33,360
His job is to harvest dates from trees that can grow to heights of

507
00:32:33,361 --> 00:32:39,360
more than 80 feet. Date grower Dennis Jensen speaks

508
00:32:39,361 --> 00:32:43,360
of the industry's anxiety over the latter harvest.

509
00:32:43,361 --> 00:32:44,360
Dennis Jensen, It's dangerous work up there.

510
00:32:44,361 --> 00:32:47,360
One slip and they could end up in a world of hurt.

511
00:32:47,361 --> 00:32:52,360
As much as we try safety-wise, it seems that every other year

512
00:32:52,460 --> 00:32:56,360
we lose someone to this terrible tragedy.

513
00:32:56,460 --> 00:32:58,800
Some of the other items that are very dangerous

514
00:32:58,900 --> 00:33:01,200
are the spines, the palm fronds themselves

515
00:33:01,300 --> 00:33:03,840
that can pierce your hand or an eye or an arm

516
00:33:03,940 --> 00:33:05,240
or a leg or foot.

517
00:33:05,340 --> 00:33:07,320
And they're located throughout the crown of that tree.

518
00:33:07,420 --> 00:33:08,620
So there's a lot of different dangers

519
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:12,320
that they have to work with.

520
00:33:12,420 --> 00:33:13,820
The palmeros in this field

521
00:33:13,920 --> 00:33:16,160
are hand-harvesting a premium date,

522
00:33:16,161 --> 00:33:19,560
known as the medjool, one of several date varieties

523
00:33:19,660 --> 00:33:22,260
that have been cultivated since before recorded time.

524
00:33:26,900 --> 00:33:29,800
Dates are likely the oldest of all harvested fruits.

525
00:33:32,340 --> 00:33:34,180
Nearly 6,000 years ago, they were

526
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:36,780
cultivated in the region of Mesopotamia,

527
00:33:36,880 --> 00:33:39,780
or what is now modern Iraq.

528
00:33:39,880 --> 00:33:43,280
Some traditions trace the fruit to the Garden of Eden.

529
00:33:43,281 --> 00:33:46,620
Even the Bible refers to the land of milk and honey,

530
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:49,780
and we've been told by Jewish scholars

531
00:33:49,880 --> 00:33:51,420
that the honey it was referring to

532
00:33:51,520 --> 00:33:54,420
was the honey of the date palms.

533
00:33:54,520 --> 00:33:56,320
Its ability to grow and produce fruit

534
00:33:56,420 --> 00:34:00,200
in such an arid environment made the date palm a sacred tree.

535
00:34:00,300 --> 00:34:03,540
Dates keep very well, and in that hot desert area

536
00:34:03,640 --> 00:34:05,540
where they had no refrigeration,

537
00:34:05,640 --> 00:34:07,040
they could keep them for a long time.

538
00:34:07,140 --> 00:34:10,240
And early on, they discovered the ways to press them together

539
00:34:10,340 --> 00:34:13,280
to get rid of air and so forth so that they would

540
00:34:13,281 --> 00:34:18,280
After many failed attempts, date palm offshoots

541
00:34:18,380 --> 00:34:22,260
were successfully imported and planted in the Arizona desert

542
00:34:22,360 --> 00:34:26,220
in the year 1900. The coveted medjool offshoots

543
00:34:26,320 --> 00:34:30,260
came from Morocco in 1927 and were later planted

544
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:32,260
in the Coachella Valley.

545
00:34:36,300 --> 00:34:38,700
Today, California growers of medjools

546
00:34:38,800 --> 00:34:42,100
and the other major variety, the deglet noor,

547
00:34:42,101 --> 00:34:45,940
oversee a thriving industry that annually harvests a crop worth

548
00:34:46,040 --> 00:34:49,840
around $50 million.

549
00:34:49,940 --> 00:34:52,240
But times are changing for the skilled palmeros.

550
00:34:54,740 --> 00:34:57,940
Most date growers are relying less on the latter harvest

551
00:34:58,040 --> 00:35:01,960
and more on a mechanical harvesting aid, the canasta.

552
00:35:05,360 --> 00:35:08,960
Mounted on a nine-ton lift, the platform opens and closes

553
00:35:09,060 --> 00:35:12,100
around the date palm's trunk.

554
00:35:12,101 --> 00:35:15,700
Even as mechanization makes the work safer and easier,

555
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:17,900
the job still requires a trained palmero

556
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:22,040
to distinguish dates that are ripe for picking.

557
00:35:22,140 --> 00:35:23,540
Normally what they're going to do

558
00:35:23,640 --> 00:35:25,540
is they'll be looking for the ripest ones,

559
00:35:25,640 --> 00:35:27,340
and they're pretty easy to pull off.

560
00:35:27,440 --> 00:35:28,860
And sometimes you can just shake them,

561
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:30,860
and they'll actually fall off of the bunches.

562
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:32,360
But they'll be pulling out the ripe ones, which

563
00:35:32,460 --> 00:35:33,560
are the darker color ones.

564
00:35:33,660 --> 00:35:36,160
There's yellow ones on here, which are not ripe yet.

565
00:35:36,260 --> 00:35:38,300
It'll take another probably two to three weeks

566
00:35:38,400 --> 00:35:39,860
before they will ripen up.

567
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:44,800
Prior to harvesting, the palmeros wrap each and every

568
00:35:44,900 --> 00:35:47,940
growing date bunch in a nylon bag.

569
00:35:48,040 --> 00:35:52,940
Our date bags are used to keep moisture away from the dates

570
00:35:53,040 --> 00:35:55,340
should we have a sudden thunderstorm.

571
00:35:55,440 --> 00:35:58,720
And the rain and moisture causes a lot of decay for the dates.

572
00:35:58,820 --> 00:36:03,060
Additional purposes of these bags are to keep insects away

573
00:36:03,160 --> 00:36:05,160
and to keep birds from eating the dates.

574
00:36:09,060 --> 00:36:11,060
To prevent damage to the delicate fruit,

575
00:36:11,061 --> 00:36:14,300
harvesters lower small baskets to the ground where they're

576
00:36:14,400 --> 00:36:15,800
hand sorted for ripeness.

577
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:23,500
After a short journey to Dennis Jensen's Seaview Packing House,

578
00:36:23,600 --> 00:36:26,800
the dates are cooled. Then they're carefully washed

579
00:36:26,900 --> 00:36:28,920
and dry before they're hand sorted for packing.

580
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:36,260
One of five quality categories are assigned before the dates

581
00:36:36,360 --> 00:36:38,720
are shipped to points around the world.

582
00:36:41,060 --> 00:36:48,060
While dates are harvested in scorching 100-degree sunlight,

583
00:36:48,061 --> 00:36:53,060
there's another crop that prefers the cool darkness of

584
00:36:53,061 --> 00:36:58,060
the world's largest underground fungus farm.

585
00:37:02,060 --> 00:37:05,060
Deep within the rolling hills near Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania,

586
00:37:05,061 --> 00:37:10,060
directly beneath the trees and pasture land, more than 800 acres

587
00:37:10,061 --> 00:37:13,760
of limestone tunnels play host to the world's largest mushroom

588
00:37:13,761 --> 00:37:14,600
farm.

589
00:37:18,600 --> 00:37:22,600
At Creekside Mushrooms, more than 150 miles of tunnels

590
00:37:22,601 --> 00:37:27,020
in a former limestone mine are a fungus farmer's dream.

591
00:37:27,021 --> 00:37:29,720
The neat thing about growing mushrooms at this particular

592
00:37:29,721 --> 00:37:33,720
facility is that the temperature is pretty much consistent all

593
00:37:33,721 --> 00:37:37,720
year round, and we maintain about a 62 degree temperature

594
00:37:37,721 --> 00:37:42,060
temperature all year round and about a 95% humidity, which

595
00:37:42,160 --> 00:37:46,600
allows mushrooms to just recycle themselves pretty much every 24

596
00:37:46,700 --> 00:37:49,500
hours.

597
00:37:49,600 --> 00:37:52,540
Creekside's fungus of choice is the sylvan

598
00:37:52,640 --> 00:37:55,400
white agaricus, the ever popular white button

599
00:37:55,500 --> 00:37:58,200
mushroom.

600
00:37:58,300 --> 00:38:00,140
Each day, a small army of harvesters

601
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:06,440
speeds from one cave to the next, each worker aiming to pick

602
00:38:06,441 --> 00:38:08,440
a minimum of 55 pounds per hour.

603
00:38:12,040 --> 00:38:13,980
The mushrooms are grown in and harvested

604
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:17,180
from large wooden trays.

605
00:38:17,280 --> 00:38:21,180
Each tray is harvested in passes or breaks.

606
00:38:21,280 --> 00:38:25,300
The first break yields the large and medium mushrooms.

607
00:38:25,400 --> 00:38:28,120
Removing the larger fungi allows more room

608
00:38:28,220 --> 00:38:31,360
for the smaller mushrooms to grow and develop.

609
00:38:31,460 --> 00:38:34,200
I'm picking the mushrooms that are

610
00:38:34,201 --> 00:38:37,540
ready at the prime time for harvest.

611
00:38:37,640 --> 00:38:41,000
And that depends upon the stem length,

612
00:38:41,100 --> 00:38:45,540
the shape of the cap, whether it's domed or oval.

613
00:38:45,640 --> 00:38:47,920
If you notice, the only smalls that I take

614
00:38:48,020 --> 00:38:51,960
are the ones that come off basically by accident.

615
00:38:52,060 --> 00:38:53,680
If they're left on there for tomorrow,

616
00:38:53,780 --> 00:38:58,160
there'll be mushrooms that will be harvested again tomorrow.

617
00:38:58,260 --> 00:39:01,920
Each tray ultimately yields approximately 250 pounds

618
00:39:01,921 --> 00:39:05,320
of organic mushrooms, but not every mushroom is perfect.

619
00:39:05,420 --> 00:39:09,020
This mushroom in particular has a blemish on it.

620
00:39:09,120 --> 00:39:11,660
That is what we consider a number two.

621
00:39:11,760 --> 00:39:14,860
It would not look as nice when it hits the supermarket.

622
00:39:20,580 --> 00:39:22,400
The popular white agaricus mushroom

623
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:25,920
was first domesticated in the cellars and sewers

624
00:39:26,020 --> 00:39:28,980
of 19th century France.

625
00:39:29,080 --> 00:39:31,920
The process of producing the spawn, or seed,

626
00:39:31,921 --> 00:39:35,920
to growing the fungus commercially.

627
00:39:36,020 --> 00:39:38,260
After the French perfected a strain of spawn,

628
00:39:38,360 --> 00:39:41,660
it was soon exported to England, and ultimately

629
00:39:41,760 --> 00:39:44,160
to the United States.

630
00:39:44,260 --> 00:39:48,160
The mushroom business took off in 1904, when the United States

631
00:39:48,260 --> 00:39:51,280
Department of Agriculture perfected a domestically bred

632
00:39:51,380 --> 00:39:54,880
spawn, one of the very same strains that are used today.

633
00:39:59,020 --> 00:40:01,680
Even with a healthy spawn, the plant was

634
00:40:01,681 --> 00:40:04,080
Profitably growing mushrooms in vast numbers

635
00:40:04,180 --> 00:40:06,580
is a very demanding craft.

636
00:40:06,680 --> 00:40:10,880
The process begins with piles of hay, straw, and manure.

637
00:40:10,980 --> 00:40:14,220
At Creekside Mushrooms, this compost is turned,

638
00:40:14,320 --> 00:40:16,920
adding water and fresh air to speed up decomposition.

639
00:40:21,140 --> 00:40:24,740
The compost is then pasteurized in a fresh air tunnel system.

640
00:40:24,840 --> 00:40:26,980
It kills all the other microorganisms

641
00:40:27,080 --> 00:40:30,980
that are in the compost so that when we do plant the spawn

642
00:40:30,981 --> 00:40:33,180
or the mushroom seeds into the tray,

643
00:40:33,280 --> 00:40:38,920
it gives a nice clean medium for the spawn to take hold

644
00:40:39,020 --> 00:40:42,220
inside the compost.

645
00:40:42,320 --> 00:40:44,220
Each tray is filled with compost,

646
00:40:44,320 --> 00:40:47,360
and the spawn is then added.

647
00:40:47,460 --> 00:40:50,240
The trays are transported deep in the pitch dark limestone

648
00:40:50,340 --> 00:40:54,000
caves to begin coaxing the root structure, or mycelium,

649
00:40:54,100 --> 00:40:54,840
to the surface.

650
00:40:54,841 --> 00:41:00,840
This tray of organic white agaricus will begin harvesting

651
00:41:00,940 --> 00:41:02,840
around day 15.

652
00:41:06,880 --> 00:41:09,280
Its mini mushrooms travel to Creekside's packaging

653
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:13,700
facility, where they're weighed, packaged, or sometimes sliced

654
00:41:13,800 --> 00:41:16,300
before they're cooled and quickly shipped to market.

655
00:41:19,340 --> 00:41:21,440
We don't get a whole lot of lead time,

656
00:41:21,540 --> 00:41:23,440
nor do we want a whole lot of lead time,

657
00:41:23,441 --> 00:41:26,240
because what we produce today, we want to make sure we ship

658
00:41:26,340 --> 00:41:29,680
within the next 24 hours.

659
00:41:29,780 --> 00:41:32,480
While the mushroom harvest may seem stable,

660
00:41:32,580 --> 00:41:34,180
its future is not.

661
00:41:34,280 --> 00:41:35,980
People that get out of high school today

662
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:38,820
look for other opportunities, so it gets harder and harder

663
00:41:38,920 --> 00:41:41,600
to find labor.

664
00:41:41,700 --> 00:41:43,800
Labor holds us back from expanding.

665
00:41:43,900 --> 00:41:47,400
Right now, we grow about 20, 26 million pounds.

666
00:41:47,500 --> 00:41:52,300
We'd like to be 30, 35, but our restraint says we can't get

667
00:41:52,301 --> 00:41:56,300
to get us to where we want to be.

668
00:41:56,400 --> 00:41:59,100
Creekside's mushrooms are not the only crop impacted

669
00:41:59,200 --> 00:42:00,300
by a lack of labor.

670
00:42:04,320 --> 00:42:08,320
The trend is affecting harvests across the nation.

671
00:42:08,420 --> 00:42:11,020
There's certainly less labor available.

672
00:42:11,120 --> 00:42:14,360
It began several years ago, but the increased pressure

673
00:42:14,460 --> 00:42:18,120
to reduce illegal immigration across the southern United

674
00:42:18,220 --> 00:42:20,820
States border has influenced the amount of laborers

675
00:42:20,821 --> 00:42:22,320
that are available for harvest.

676
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:28,820
As workers become scarcer, mechanization

677
00:42:28,920 --> 00:42:31,940
will play a larger but probably still restricted role

678
00:42:32,040 --> 00:42:34,000
in the harvest.

679
00:42:34,100 --> 00:42:37,580
Labor is the most important factor in this business.

680
00:42:37,680 --> 00:42:40,640
When you look at the fragility or the nature of the fruit,

681
00:42:40,740 --> 00:42:42,580
this is not something that is conducive

682
00:42:42,680 --> 00:42:46,240
to mechanical harvesting.

683
00:42:46,340 --> 00:42:48,920
We rely very heavily on a labor force that's skilled

684
00:42:48,921 --> 00:42:50,960
to discern fruit quality on the tree.

685
00:42:51,060 --> 00:42:54,260
And there's really no other way to go about doing that.

686
00:42:56,420 --> 00:42:59,260
Surely our machines will become more sophisticated

687
00:42:59,360 --> 00:43:01,260
and sensitive.

688
00:43:01,360 --> 00:43:03,360
And a declining labor force will spur

689
00:43:03,460 --> 00:43:05,880
that mechanical innovation.

690
00:43:05,980 --> 00:43:09,080
But only time will tell if the machine will ever match

691
00:43:09,180 --> 00:43:13,320
the human hand's ability to delicately pluck an unblemished

692
00:43:13,420 --> 00:43:14,320
piece of fruit.

